course: adult-life
lesson: 3
title: Finding a job and getting on
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Getting a job is a bit like trying out for a team: you send in your details, you go for a try-out, and often someone else gets picked.
explain: Grown-ups write a CV, send applications, go to interviews, wait on probation in a new job, and later hope for a pay rise or a promotion.
explain: Picture a flat in Toronto: every evening Mum sits at the laptop sending off applications, gets "no" after "no", and then one Tuesday bursts through the door shouting "I got it!" while the whole family cheers.
explain: Being turned down is normal for everyone, and being picked is the reward for trying again and again.
explain: At the other end of working life comes retirement, when people stop paid work for good.
fact: Job hunting means searching adverts and sending applications, often dozens before getting anywhere; many get no reply at all.
fact: A CV is a page or two listing someone's education, jobs and skills; Americans call it a résumé.
fact: Employers often glance at a CV for less than a minute, so grown-ups fuss over every word.
fact: A job interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions to decide whom to hire; several people usually compete for one job.
fact: "They went with someone with more experience" is the polite way of saying someone didn't get the job; often nothing was wrong with them.
fact: A promotion is a move up to a more senior job in the same organisation, usually with more responsibility and more pay.
fact: A promotion can also bring more stress, longer hours, or being in charge of people who used to be equals.
fact: A pay rise is an increase in pay; some come each year, others have to be asked for.
fact: If prices rise and pay doesn't, a person is really getting poorer each year.
fact: Probation at work is a trial period, often three or six months, when the worker and employer see if the job fits; it has nothing to do with crime.
fact: A reference is a short message from a past employer or teacher saying the person was reliable; it is why grown-ups try to leave jobs politely.
fact: Retiring means stopping paid work for good, usually in your sixties, and living on a pension and savings.
fact: Some retired people miss the routine, their workmates and the feeling of being useful.
fact: Starting a new job means learning new systems, names and rules all at once, so people come home tired.
exercise: Mum is at the laptop every evening: "I'm applying for jobs." What is she doing? | Searching adverts and sending applications | Playing a game about jobs | Doing her homework for night school | Ordering things online
exercise: Mum has sent twenty applications and heard nothing. What does that mean? | It is normal, many applications get no reply | She must be useless at everything | The internet is broken | Nobody needs workers any more
exercise: "Can you check my CV for spelling mistakes?" What is a CV? | A page listing someone's education, jobs and skills | A letter from the bank about how much money is left | A form for a new passport | A list of everything bought last month
exercise: Why do grown-ups fuss over every word of a CV? | Employers often look at it for less than a minute | The government keeps every CV forever | Each spelling mistake costs a fine | CVs are read aloud on the radio
exercise: What do Americans call a CV? | A résumé | A reference | A payslip | A contract
exercise: Dad is in a tie on a weekday: "Wish me luck, it's an interview." What is a job interview? | A meeting where an employer asks questions to decide whom to hire | A chat with a reporter who wants to put him in the Sunday newspaper | A test to get into a university | A meeting with the bank about borrowing money to buy a new car
exercise: Dad is a really good worker but didn't get the job. What is the most likely reason? | Several people competed and another fitted better | He must have been rude to everybody at the interview | Employers only hire their relatives | Good workers are never hired
exercise: "They went with someone with more experience." What does it mean? | She didn't get the job | She got the job | She is going to work with someone new | She needs to find someone to go with
exercise: Mum bursts through the door: "I got the promotion!" What has happened? | She is moving up to a more senior job with more pay | She has been moved to a different firm in another town | She won a prize in a draw at work | She is going on a long holiday
exercise: After her promotion, Mum is busier and more tired. Why? | More responsibility can mean more stress and longer hours | She must have been demoted to a worse job | She doesn't like her new pay | She is ill and should stop working
exercise: "I'm going to ask for a pay rise." What is that? | Asking for more pay | Asking to be paid earlier | Asking for a lift to work | Asking to work fewer hours
exercise: Why is it worth asking for a pay rise when prices go up? | If prices rise and pay doesn't, you are really getting poorer | The boss will be offended and upset if nobody ever asks for more | A pay rise lowers the prices in all the shops in the town | Asking is the law every year
exercise: "I'm still on probation at the new place." What does probation mean here? | A trial period in a new job | Being in trouble with the police | Being on holiday | Training to be a judge
exercise: "My old boss has agreed to give me a reference." What is a reference? | A message from a past employer saying the person was reliable | A book you look things up in | A gift for leaving a job, like a card or flowers | A list of all the jobs that are free in the whole town this week
exercise: At Grandad's leaving party: "Forty years and they gave him a clock." What is Grandad doing? | Retiring, stopping paid work for good | Moving to a new job across town | Starting a clock repair business | Going on a business trip
exercise: Dad after his first day somewhere new: "My head is spinning." Why? | He is learning lots of new names, systems and rules at once | He is dizzy because he is ill | He spun round in his office chair | He was given a promotion by mistake
exercise: What is a kind question for Dad after his first day? | Ask for three good things about the day | Ask whether he has been sacked yet | Ask how much he earns now | Ask why he took such a hard job
test: Overheard: "Fifty applications. Fifty. Two replies, both no." What should you think? | Hearing "no" many times is normal, keep encouraging her | She must be applying for jobs in the wrong country by mistake | The firms are all closing down | She is not trying hard enough
test: Your brother says: "Probation? So Mum's in trouble with the cops?" Put him right. | At work, probation is just a trial period in a new job | He is right, probation means a police check on her whole family | Probation means she failed her first week | Probation means she isn't paid yet
test: Dad on the phone: "Yeah, three of us shortlisted, final round Thursday." What is happening? | Three people are competing at interview for one job | Dad has been offered three jobs | Dad is choosing between three colleagues | Dad has already got the job and he starts on Thursday morning
test: Mum, flat: "Nah, didn't get it. 'Strong field', apparently." What is the kind response? | A hug and "their loss" | "Maybe you weren't good enough" | "Why do you even bother" | "Never mind, just stop looking"
test: Overheard: "Don't burn your bridges, you'll need a reference." What does the advice mean? | Leave politely, a future employer may ask your old boss about you | Never cross a bridge on the way to work | Keep your old job as well as the new one, just in case it goes wrong | Don't argue with people who live nearby
test: Grandma: "Since Grandad retired he's driving me round the bend, he misses work." Why might Grandad be restless? | He misses the routine, his workmates and feeling useful | He has to work twice as hard now that he is at home all day | He lost all his money when he retired | He was sacked and is ashamed
test: Mum: "Promoted, yeah, but now I'm managing Sue, and we were mates." Why is that hard? | She is now in charge of someone who used to be her equal | Sue was promoted above her | She has been moved to a smaller job with a lot less pay than before | Sue has been sacked
test: Dad: "Everything in the shop's gone up, my pay hasn't. Great." What does he mean? | His pay buys less than last year, so he is really poorer | He is richer now, because prices went up and so did everything else | His pay went up just as much as the prices did | The shops are giving discounts
test: Your sister: "If you get turned down it means you're rubbish." Put her right. | Often nothing was wrong, another person just fitted better | She is right, only bad workers are ever turned down for jobs | It means the employer made a mistake on purpose | It means you must never apply again
test: Your aunt in Florida tells Dad: "Send me your résumé." What does she want? | His CV, listing his jobs and skills | His payslip | A reference from his boss | A photo of his office
test: Mum before an interview: "Haven't slept, so nervous." What is true? | Nerves before an interview are completely normal | Being nervous means she will not get the job | Only bad candidates feel nervous before an interview | Interviews are nothing to be nervous about
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a probationary period in employment? | A trial period in which employer and employee assess the suitability of the appointment | A period of supervision imposed by a court following a criminal offence of any description | A period of unpaid leave granted after illness | A period following retirement during which a pension is withheld
exam: Identify the principal function of a reference supplied by a former employer. | To attest to the reliability and competence of the applicant | To specify the salary the applicant must be paid by the new employer | To list all vacancies currently available | To record the applicant's medical history
exam: Given that wages remain unchanged whilst prices rise, determine the effect upon the employee. | The employee's pay purchases less, and the employee is in effect poorer | The employee's pay purchases more, and the employee is accordingly richer | The employee's position remains wholly unaltered | The employee becomes entitled to a promotion
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning promotion. | A promotion confers a more senior position, generally with greater responsibility and pay | A promotion is a reduction in duties granted in recognition of long and faithful service to the firm | A promotion is the transfer of an employee to another organisation | A promotion is a payment made upon retirement
